\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

Gradient analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}, Appendix) examines the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only).

\textbf{Gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict.} Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity is near zero across all encoder layers for both Full OCC and Random OCC, with conflict rates of ${\sim}$50\%. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions, ruling out gradient amplification. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, consistent with the behavioral finding that spatial content does not affect outcomes.

\textbf{Mechanism remains open.} The observed interaction pattern cannot be explained by gradient conflict (orthogonality rules it out) or gradient amplification (norms are comparable). The detrimental effect likely operates through indirect modification of the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape. CKA analysis~\cite{kornblith2019similarity} confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP), localizing the safety degradation to optimization dynamics rather than representational corruption.

Additional observations (per-task breakdown, depth comparison, and temporal dynamics) are in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_gradient}

BatchNorm is not inherently harmful---Detach OCC (BN, gradient OFF) is indistinguishable from Vanilla ($d{=}-0.04$). The damage emerges only when auxiliary gradients flow through the shared encoders. The mechanism through which BatchNorm amplifies this deficit remains unknown: CKA analysis confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP), ruling out representational drift as the causal pathway. The relative contribution of normalization type depends on the sample: preliminary data show both BN and LN variants degrade under gradient flow ($-$4.2\,pp and $-$2.5\,pp respectively), but the LN cells have only $n{=}1$ each, and the interaction direction requires larger samples to confirm. What is clear is that the BN + gradient flow cell is the most unstable configuration ($\sigma{=}0.064$, $>$2.5${\times}$ the gradient-OFF cell). One hypothesis is that BatchNorm's running statistics, computed over the joint activation distribution, introduce sensitivity to distributional shifts that affects downstream action decoding without altering representational geometry. LayerNorm, which normalizes per-token independently of the batch, may dampen this sensitivity.

Gradient analysis confirms orthogonality between action and auxiliary gradients (cosine $\approx$ 0, conflict rate $\approx$ 50\%), ruling out direct gradient conflict~\cite{yu2020pcgrad, liu2021cagrad}. The detrimental effect operates through indirect channels---likely reshaping the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape~\cite{lippl2024implicit, jiang2023forkmerge}---rather than through gradient interference. This sensitivity extends to implementation details. A codebase revision (v2) that fixed a batch-index resume bug and restructured the auxiliary head pipeline---without changing any model architecture, hyperparameter, or evaluation protocol---shifts absolute CAR by up to 8.6\,pp for the same seed, while the Vanilla baseline remains stable ($<$1.5\,pp shift). More strikingly, the normalization $\times$ gradient flow interaction pattern itself changed from an apparent crossover under v1 to the ordinal pattern reported here under v2 (Appendix~\ref{app:codebase}). This implementation sensitivity is itself a finding: auxiliary spatial heads introduce an optimization fragility in which safety outcomes depend on code-level details (module initialization order, data loading sequence) that are rarely specified or reproduced across studies, strengthening the case against adding auxiliary heads for safety purposes.

These findings imply that spatial VLA methods~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, yuan2025depthvla, wu2025spatialvla, liu2025occvla} must report safety metrics alongside task success, since auxiliary gradient flow can degrade collision avoidance even when task success is maintained. Normalization type and gradient flow configuration---rarely specified in current work---should be standard reporting practice.

Seven of ten tasks already reach CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla (ceiling effect); safety differences concentrate on three physically constrained tasks where collisions arise from proximity, not spatial unawareness (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\textbf{Practical guideline.} Do not add auxiliary spatial heads for the purpose of improving collision avoidance; all tested configurations perform at or below the Vanilla baseline. If an auxiliary head is required by other objectives, use detached gradients with LayerNorm (Detach+LN), and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow. No ground-truth spatial labels are needed; the head is discarded at zero inference cost, complementing runtime safety methods~\cite{hu2025vlsa, raja2024ogmcbf}. Limitations are discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented a systematic factorial study asking whether auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning improves collision avoidance. The answer is no: all twelve configurations perform at or below the no-auxiliary-head baseline. Effect sizes from a $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) identify the conditions under which auxiliary tasks hurt: gradient flow through the auxiliary head is associated with lower safety under both normalization types ($\Delta{=}-4.2$\,pp within BatchNorm, $-$2.5\,pp within LayerNorm), and the BN + gradient flow cell is the most unstable configuration ($\sigma$ more than $2.5{\times}$ other cells; whether normalization type modulates the magnitude requires additional seeds to confirm), and spatial label content is irrelevant to the outcome. The precise mechanism remains open---gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict, pointing instead to indirect effects on the LoRA optimization trajectory---but the behavioral pattern is robust across all conditions tested. Guidelines: do not add auxiliary spatial heads for safety; if required by other objectives, use Detach+LN and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow (no ground-truth labels needed, zero inference cost).

CKA analysis confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95), localizing the safety degradation to the optimization trajectory rather than representational capacity. More broadly, training-time collision avoidance may require approaches that directly encode safety constraints~\cite{zhang2025safevla} rather than auxiliary prediction tasks that improve spatial awareness without shaping the policy's collision behavior.

\section{Method}

We fine-tune OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft}, which concatenates DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024dinov2} (1024-d) and SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} (1152-d) patch features into a 2176-dimensional representation per spatial token. We apply LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} (rank 32, $\alpha{=}16$) and augment the action prediction loss with an auxiliary spatial prediction head:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda(t) \cdot \mathcal{L}_\text{aux}(h(\phi_v(o_t)), s_t^*),
\quad \lambda(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t / 50000)
\end{equation}
where $h$ denotes the auxiliary head, $\phi_v$ the frozen-then-LoRA-adapted vision encoders, and $s_t^*$ the ground-truth spatial label at step $t$. The coefficient $\lambda(t)$ decays exponentially so that spatial gradients shape early representations without dominating late-stage action refinement. The auxiliary head is discarded at inference, incurring zero additional cost.

\textbf{BEV occupancy head.} The head (1.2M parameters) maps the 256 patch tokens from the current observation frame to a $64{\times}64$ binary occupancy grid. A linear projection ($2176{\to}256$) is reshaped to $16{\times}16$, followed by two transposed convolutions ($256{\to}128{\to}64$, kernel 4, stride 2) each with BatchNorm2d and GELU, and a final $1{\times}1$ convolution with sigmoid activation. Ground-truth grids are rendered from MuJoCo simulator state replay by projecting object bounding boxes onto the table-plane BEV. Training uses focal loss~\cite{lin2017focal} ($\alpha{=}0.25$, $\gamma{=}2.0$) to handle class imbalance in the sparse occupancy signal. The initial auxiliary weight $\lambda_0{=}0.01$ is set to keep auxiliary gradients below 2\% of the action loss magnitude, avoiding interference with action learning.

\textbf{Monocular depth head.} This head shares the same convolutional architecture but replaces the sigmoid with ReLU and uses smooth-L1 loss. Ground-truth egocentric depth maps are rendered at $64{\times}64$ resolution from MuJoCo. The depth head provides an egocentric spatial reference frame, complementing the allocentric BEV occupancy grid.

\subsection{Ablation Design}
\label{sec:ablation}

We evaluate twelve conditions that systematically vary five factors: gradient flow from the auxiliary head into the vision encoders, label content, normalization type in the auxiliary head, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent of auxiliary supervision.

The key conditions are: \textbf{Vanilla} (action loss only, no auxiliary head); \textbf{Full OCC} (BEV occupancy with gradient flow through both encoders, BatchNorm2d); \textbf{Detach OCC} (same labels, encoder gradients blocked via \texttt{stop\_gradient}); and their LayerNorm variants (\textbf{Full OCC+LN}, \textbf{Detach+LN}). These four form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization). Additional conditions include \textbf{Random OCC} (random binary labels, isolating gradient regularization from spatial content), \textbf{Depth} (egocentric depth maps), \textbf{Cutoff@\{15K--45K\}} (gradient flow for the first $N$ steps only; the post-cutoff phase retains the auxiliary head forward pass in Detach mode, see Appendix~\ref{app:training}), and \textbf{Reverse Cutoff} (gradient flow for the last 15K steps). Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design} summarizes the full design and the nine pairwise comparisons, each isolating a single factor.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\caption{Ablation design: each comparison isolates one factor.}
\label{tab:ablation_design}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Comparison & Isolated Factor \\
\midrule
Full OCC vs.\ Vanilla & Auxiliary spatial supervision \\
Random vs.\ Full OCC & Spatial content vs.\ gradient flow \\
Detach vs.\ Vanilla & Encoder gradient flow (presence/absence) \\
Detach+LN vs.\ Detach OCC & Normalization (BN vs.\ LN) without gradient flow \\
Full OCC+LN vs.\ Full OCC & Normalization (BN vs.\ LN) with gradient flow \\
$2{\times}2$ factorial & Normalization $\times$ gradient flow interaction \\
Depth vs.\ Full OCC & Egocentric vs.\ allocentric frame \\
Cutoff@\{15K--45K\} vs.\ Full OCC & Temporal extent of gradient flow \\
Reverse Cutoff vs.\ Cutoff@35K & Early vs.\ late gradient flow \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, which places obstacles in close proximity to manipulation targets across 10 tabletop tasks drawn from LIBERO-Spatial~\cite{liu2023libero}. We report two metrics: \textbf{Task Success Rate (TSR)}, the fraction of episodes in which the task is completed, and \textbf{Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR)}, where a collision is registered when the L1 displacement of any obstacle \texttt{body\_xpos} exceeds $10^{-3}$. The five core factorial conditions (Vanilla, Full OCC, Full OCC+LN, Detach OCC, Detach+LN), along with Random OCC, Depth, and Cutoff@35K, each use 1--4 independent seeds; remaining temporal conditions (Cutoff@\{15K, 25K, 45K\}, Reverse Cutoff) use 1--2 seeds as noted in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}. Each seed runs 50 rollouts per task (500 episodes per seed).

The BN factorial cells (Full OCC and Detach OCC) have been fully retrained under a revised codebase (v2, 3--4 seeds each); the LN cells and additional Vanilla seeds are in progress at the time of writing. The $2{\times}2$ ANOVA uses v2 data for the BN row and v1 data for the LN row; this will be updated to fully v2 data before final submission. Supplementary conditions use the original codebase (v1). Both versions share identical architectures, hyperparameters, and evaluation protocols. Bootstrap 95\% CIs use 10{,}000 hierarchical resamples (percentile method). Training runs for 50K steps on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU; full details are in Appendix~\ref{app:training}.

